"hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1624,"Narrated to us Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn ‘Abbad ibn ‘Abbad al-Muhallabi, Abu Talib Zayd ibn Akhzam al-Ta’i, and Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Azdi, who said: Narrated to us ‘Abd Allah ibn Dawud, who said: Narrated to us Salamah ibn Nubayt, from Nu‘aym ibn Abi Hind, from Nubayt ibn Sharit, from Salim ibn ‘Ubayd, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill, and he lost consciousness. Then he regained consciousness and asked, “Has the time for prayer come?”
We said, “Yes.”
He said, “Instruct Bilal to give the call to prayer, and instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.”

Then he lost consciousness again, and then regained it, asking again, “Has the time for prayer come?”
We said, “Yes.”
He said, “Instruct Bilal to give the call to prayer, and instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.”

Then he lost consciousness again, and then regained it. ‘A’ishah said, “Indeed, my father is a soft-hearted man. If only you had instructed someone else.”

He regained consciousness and asked, “Has the time for prayer come?”
We said, “Yes.”
He said, “Instruct Bilal to give the call to prayer, and instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.”

‘A’ishah said, “Indeed, my father is a soft-hearted man. If only you had instructed someone else.”

He said, “Indeed, you are like the companions of Yusuf (Joseph). Instruct Bilal to give the call to prayer, and instruct Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.”

Then he lost consciousness again. So they instructed Bilal to give the call to prayer and establish it, and they instructed Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.

Then he regained consciousness and asked, “Has the prayer been established?”
We said, “Yes.”
He said, “Bring me a man to lean upon.”

So they brought him Barirah and another man, and he leaned upon them. Then he went out to the prayer, and he was seated beside Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr began to step back, but the Prophet (ﷺ) held him until he completed the prayer.

This is the narration of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad.

**CHAPTER:**"
